        "cost_usd": 0.089942,
        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is small, additive, and idiomatic:\n- A dedicated `AUDIT` logger declared as `private static final` via SLF4J \u2014 the standard Spring/Java logging idiom, and it correctly matches the spec's required logger name `\"AUDIT\"`.\n- The emit is placed right after `clinicService.saveOwner(owner)`, which is exactly the \"successful create\" point, and it logs the three required fields (owner id, customerCode, registrationDate) using parameterized SLF4J placeholders rather than string concatenation.\n- Nothing is duplicated, and the controller isn't turning into a catch-all \u2014 this is one cohesive concern (audit on create) sitting alongside the existing create flow. A maintainer would find the placement and style natural.\n\nOne could debate whether audit logging belongs in the service layer, but given the spec frames it at the create-request boundary and the controller already owns create-side concerns here (e.g. `CITY_CAPACITY`, namesake counting), keeping it in the controller is a defensible, consistent choice \u2014 not a structural problem."
      }
